  {
    "episode_id": 49881,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49881.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "There is kind of a syndrome where we only pay attention to national politics because, you know, it's the headline news on our national and we've had a reduction in local news, as you know. And it really has handicapped our ability to understand how you can make progress at a state level. We on the pro choice. pro-environment, pro-working family side of some of these debates are sort of awakening to that fact and understanding how important state legislative races are and governor's races. Certainly the governor's races are important and state legislators on a national basis too because of the gerrymandering that goes on at the state that affects the Congress as well. So on multiple of these issues, including this one, here's a way to advance a woman's right, this constitutional right of freedom. It is a freedom right. This is the ultimate intrusion by government ordering a person what to do with their body. That is the ultimate intrusion in a person's freedom and liberty. for control of their own lives. And so this is maybe the most important freedom that now we must protect. So I think your point is states are extremely important. Every vote is critical up and down the ballot. And we're awakening to this, frankly. We sort of got the Republicans for to get the jump on us of maybe a decade or two ago on this long term effort to strip away a woman's rights We're now engaged in that. The 20 governors that you've talked about is one indication of that. So voting up and down the ticket is pretty important.",
